    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic stress disorder, improving with Cognitive Processing Therapy (F43.10)</content>. PCL-5 fell from 42 to 31, an 11-point drop that exceeds the 10 points usually taken as a clinically meaningful change. A score of 31 is still at the edge of the 31 to 33 range that suggests probable PTSD, so the full course is needed. Dr. Lee&apos;s evaluation confirms the diagnosis. [S]<list><item>Complete the Cognitive Processing Therapy course with Dr. Lee, aiming for at least eight sessions by the twelve-week review.</item><item>No PTSD medication, in line with his preference and Dr. Lee&apos;s advice; revisit only if the response stalls.</item><item>Repeat the PCL-5 and PHQ-9 at the twelve-week review.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Depressive symptoms</content>. PHQ-9 fell from 11 to 7. Dr. Lee&apos;s interview today found that the low mood, poor sleep, and poor concentration are part of the PTSD, with no separate major depressive episode and no history of mania or hypomania. [S]<list><item>No separate depression treatment.</item><item>PHQ-9 continues at each review alongside the PCL-5.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic insomnia, improving (G47.00)</content>. Sleep onset about 30 minutes and nightmares two or three nights a week, with the sleep routine used on five to six nights a week, which meets the six-week sleep goal. No symptoms of sleep apnea. [S]<list><item>Continue the sleep routine, stimulus control, and a fixed wake time.</item><item>No sleep medication, and no over-the-counter sedating sleep aids.</item><item>Offer cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia if the insomnia persists after the trauma-focused course.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Suicide risk review</content>. No suicidal ideation, intent, plan, or preparatory behavior since intake on a structured since-last-visit screen. Protective factors are unchanged: engagement in treatment, family and peer supports, and purposeful work and service identity. Acute risk remains low and outpatient care remains appropriate. [S]<list><item>Safety plan reviewed with him and unchanged; the lethal-means steps from intake remain in place.</item><item>Crisis resources reconfirmed, including the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline (press 1 for the Veterans Crisis Line).</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PCL-5</td><td>Baseline, then at set intervals during treatment (care plan: six and twelve weeks)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>31, down 11 points from 42 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline; at least monthly during active treatment; periodically in recovery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7, down from 11 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 recheck about four weeks after a baseline score of 10 or more</td><td>Monthly during active treatment</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not done. The care plan set checks at six and twelve weeks, so the intake score of 11 waited six weeks instead of about four, a deviation from the CMID schedule. Today&apos;s score of 7 shows no harm resulted, and Dr. Lee&apos;s interview today found no separate depressive disorder, so the PHQ-9 now follows the PCL-5 schedule</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Behavioral health consultation and diagnostic evaluation</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dr. Lee&apos;s report received today: PTSD confirmed; no separate major depressive episode and no history of mania or hypomania, so a bipolar screen (Mood Disorder Questionnaire) is not needed</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Trauma-focused psychotherapy (Cognitive Processing Therapy)</td><td>Weekly course of about 12 sessions</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Five sessions attended; on track for the goal of eight by the twelve-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS screener, since last visit)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Negative: no ideation, intent, plan, or behavior since intake</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Safety plan, lethal-means steps, and crisis resources</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline reconfirmed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Alcohol and other substance use</td><td>At intake and during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None, consistent with the intake social history</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep diary and agreed sleep routine</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Routine used five to six nights a week; sleep onset about 30 minutes; six-week goal met</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Obstructive sleep apnea symptom screen</td><td>At the insomnia assessment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not documented at the intake visit, a gap in the insomnia assessment; done today: no snoring, witnessed apneas, or daytime sleepiness and BMI 27.3, so sleep testing is not indicated</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>PTSD medication and sedative-hypnotics</td><td>If psychotherapy is declined or the response stalls</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>He chose trauma-focused psychotherapy first and is responding. No benzodiazepines, which the PTSD guideline advises against, and no sleep medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Mental status examination, vital signs, and weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Affect brighter and less constricted; BP 128/82 mmHg; weight 74.2 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Laboratory tests</td><td>Only for medication monitoring or a suspected medical cause</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No medicines; neither protocol calls for routine laboratory tests</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reduce PCL-5 score from 42 to 27 or lower by the twelve-week review. In progress; target 1964-09-30 [S]</item><item>Complete at least eight Cognitive Processing Therapy sessions by the twelve-week review. In progress; target 1964-09-30 [S]</item><item>Use the agreed sleep routine on at least five nights each week by the six-week review. Outcome by 1964-08-11: Patient-reported use on five to six nights each week [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Acute Stress Disorder (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder and Obstructive Sleep Apnea (2025); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Cognitive Processing Therapy continues weekly with Dr. Lee. Twelve-week review with the PCL-5 and PHQ-9. Use the safety plan and call the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline (press 1) at any time if needed. Next scheduled visit 1964-09-30: three-month care-plan review. [S]</paragraph></text>

